# elohim/holochain/dna — DNA sources (hash-neutrality gate)

Integrity and coordinator zomes plus each DNA's `dna.yaml`. One pipeline builds every DNA
(`elohim/holochain/dna/Jenkinsfile`); a DNA subdirectory holding only `dna.yaml` + `zomes/` + a
`justfile` is normal and fully covered — the absence of a per-DNA Jenkinsfile is not missing CI.

## The one asymmetry this manifest gates

**The DNA hash covers integrity zomes + modifiers. Coordinator zomes are not in it.**

| Diff touches | Hash | How it reaches a running conductor |
|---|---|---|
| `*/zomes/*_integrity/**` | **moves** | reinstall — `ALLOW_DNA_REINSTALL`; mints a new agent key; partial application splits the DHT |
| the hashed `integrity:` slice of `*/dna.yaml` (modifiers) | **moves** | same as above |
| `*/zomes/<coordinator>/**` | unchanged | `happ_manager::sync_coordinators` → `update_coordinators` hot-swap; no re-key, no churn |

The `dna-hash-neutrality` inject fires **only on the hash-moving classes** and labels the verdict
with its consequence (the reinstall/partition trap). Coordinator-only edits pass silently: the
hot-swap path has no trap, and an advisory on every coordinator write would train agents to ignore
the one that matters. For `dna.yaml` the validator compares the *hashed slice* of the pre- and
post-edit file — a coordinator-side or cosmetic edit to that file does not fire.

This is where a shipped DNA fix most often fails to land: when a fix doesn't reach the fleet, the
first question is which zome class the diff touched, not whether the pipeline ran.

## Why this rule names a validator when most inject rules should not

A `validator:` key that names a detector the resolver does not implement is the concern canon's own
C7 violation — advertise ≠ serve — and the 2026-08-02 sweep dropped every such phantom key in the
repo. This one is the opposite case: `epr:validator-dna-hash-neutrality` is **registered**
(`.claude/scripts/_lib/epr_meta.py`, `REFERENCE_VALIDATORS`) and evaluated on every write beneath
this directory. It is deliberately the one registered validator with **no concern-canon row** in
either registry home: the ceremony tested DNA-hash-neutrality as a canon class and declined it —
the classification has no runtime predicate, so it is policy *machinery*, bound inline here, not a
guarantee the canon states. Machinery without canon is legitimate; canon without machinery is what
the honesty rule forbids.

See: `elohim/holochain/dna/CLAUDE.md`, `elohim/holochain/dna/SCHEMA_VERSIONS.md`, and
`genesis/docs/content/elohim-protocol/history/2026-07-12-substrate-convergence-five-defect-arc.md`.
